  "track.animation.m.acs.title": "animateContentSize measures the child, reports the animating size",
  "track.animation.m.acs.overview": "A Box 40 px tall (illustrative) changes to 160 px when tapped. Three versions: `animateContentSize()` placed BEFORE `height(...)` as the docs say; placed AFTER `height(...)`; and changing your mind mid-way (collapsing while it opens). Watch the size the child measures and the size the node reports to its parent.",
  "track.animation.m.acs.wrapup": "`animateContentSize` is a layout modifier: on every measure pass it measures the child, takes the child's size as the target, then reports the animation's CURRENT size to the parent (the child is clipped to that frame by `clipToBounds`). The animation value is state read during measurement, so each frame only layout re-runs, not composition. If a size modifier sits outside it, fixed constraints force the result to the new size at once, so you see no motion.",
  "track.animation.m.acs.simp.1": "The 40/160 px heights and the per-frame values (85, 128, 152…) are illustrative; the default spring (StiffnessMediumLow) gives a different real path, and the real number of frames depends on the device.",
  "track.animation.m.acs.simp.2": "The composition/layout counters count only this Box; the parent and neighbours also re-measure/re-place for the new size, which the model does not count separately.",
  "track.animation.m.acs.ctl.hint": "Run all three and compare the \"reported to parent\" column with the \"child measured\" column.",
  "track.animation.m.acs.ctl.which": "Version",
  "track.animation.m.acs.ctl.which.expand": "animateContentSize before height",
  "track.animation.m.acs.ctl.which.after": "animateContentSize after height",
  "track.animation.m.acs.ctl.which.interrupt": "Change your mind mid-way",
  "track.animation.m.acs.diagramTitle": "State, child content, the animateContentSize node, the parent",
  "track.animation.m.acs.diagramDesc": "The expanded state changes the child's height; the node measures the child and reports the animating size to the parent",
